@node Network
@chapter Network
@cindex network scan
@cindex open ports

The @dfn{Network} screen lists the devices on the local network and the
services each of them offers.  Every ten minutes raspdash tries a TCP
connection to a list of well-known ports on every address of its own
subnet, then shows one row per device: its address, a name, its MAC
address and the open ports with the service names.  For SSH it adds the
server's version from its greeting (@samp{OpenSSH_8.4p1
Raspbian-5+deb11u7}), and for plain HTTP ports the @samp{Server} header
of the answer to a @samp{HEAD} request.  The machine raspdash runs on
is marked with @samp{★}.  The frame title gives the subnet, the
interface, the number of devices, how long ago the scan ran and how long
it took.

The name of a device is the name of the @code{[machines]} host with the
same address (the @code{local} host for raspdash's own machine) or, if
none, the name the network's DNS server gives the address; @samp{?}
means neither is known.

@section How devices are found

The scan uses ordinary TCP connections, so it needs no root privileges
and no external tool such as @command{nmap}.  It keeps up to
@code{parallel} connections in flight and gives each @code{timeout_ms}
milliseconds; a /24 network with the default 22 ports takes 10 to 15
seconds.  A port is open when the connection succeeds.  A device is
listed when the kernel's ARP table (@file{/proc/net/arp}) has a complete
entry for it after the scan, which happens for every device that
answered the network at all, even one that refuses or ignores every
port (a phone, for example).  Requiring the ARP entry also discards
answers that a local proxy or VPN fakes for addresses where nothing
exists.  When @code{subnet} names a network the Pi is not on, there is
no ARP to rely on, and every address that answered is listed.

UDP services (DNS over UDP, mDNS, DHCP) are not probed; DNS shows up
only when the device also answers DNS over TCP on port 53.

The last scan is kept in the cache directory and shown at once after a
restart.

@section Configuration

@example
[network]
interval = 600
timeout_ms = 700
parallel = 256
# subnet = "192.168.1.0/24"
# ports = [ @{ port = 22, name = "ssh" @}, @{ port = 80, name = "http" @} ]
@end example

@table @code
@item interval
Seconds between scans.  Default 600.
@item timeout_ms
How long each connection attempt may take.  Default 700; raise it on a
slow Wi-Fi network if devices are missing.
@item parallel
Connection attempts in flight at once, at most 900.  Default 256.
@item subnet
The network to scan, as @samp{@var{address}/@var{prefix}}.  Default:
the subnet of the interface that carries the default route.  Subnets
larger than a /22 are scanned as the /24 around the address.
@item ports
The ports to try, as inline tables with @code{port} and @code{name}.
Default: ftp 21, ssh 22, telnet 23, dns 53, http 80, netbios 139, https
443, smb 445, afp 548, ipp 631, mqtt 1883, rdp 3389, upnp 5000, vnc
5900, airplay 7000, proxy 7890, http-alt 8080, home-assistant 8123,
https-alt 8443, printer 9100, plex 32400 and iphone-sync 62078.  Port
names that start with @samp{http} but not @samp{https} get the
@samp{Server} header probe.
@end table

@section Internals

@file{src/network.cpp} holds the scanner, the source and the screen;
the parsing helpers are declared in @file{src/network.hpp} so that
@file{tests/test_network.cpp} can test them.  @code{probe_all} keeps a
queue of address and port pairs and a window of non-blocking sockets,
waits on them with @code{poll} until the nearest deadline, and records
each pair as open, refused or silent.  After the ARP table is read, one
thread per device looks up the DNS name with @code{getnameinfo} and
fetches the SSH greeting and HTTP headers, each within 1.5 seconds.
The result is saved as @file{network-scan.json}.

@code{util::primary_ipv4} picks the interface named on the default
route in @file{/proc/net/route}, falling back to the first IPv4
interface that is up; the header address (@pxref{Application
settings}) and the local card on the Machines screen use it too.
